 ## Purpose and scope
 
 This standard holds the practice of concurrent execution that is the same in every language and every parallel programming model: threads, tasks, coroutines, asynchronous operations, event loops, pools of workers, and the processes of one run. It adds to DSGN, VV, REPRO, INTPR, RSRC, PRIME.4, HW, and NUMER only what concurrency makes necessary. It covers the failures of concurrent tasks (STND.CONC.1 and STND.CONC.2); shared state (STND.CONC.3, STND.CONC.4, STND.CONC.5, and STND.CONC.6); locks and waits (STND.CONC.7, STND.CONC.8, STND.CONC.9, STND.CONC.10, and STND.CONC.11); the lifecycle of tasks, cancellation, queues, pools, and event loops (STND.CONC.12, STND.CONC.13, STND.CONC.14, STND.CONC.15, and STND.CONC.16); state that applies to a whole process or node (STND.CONC.17, STND.CONC.18, and STND.CONC.19); blocking in constrained targets (STND.CONC.20); results that depend on the number of tasks (STND.CONC.21, STND.CONC.22, and STND.CONC.23); verification (STND.CONC.24, STND.CONC.25, STND.CONC.26, STND.CONC.27, and STND.CONC.28); and the record of operations left pending at the end of a run (STND.CONC.29).
 
