 The rules on locks and waits are those that every coding standard in the research note shares (c2, section 3). A deadlock among locks needs a cycle in the order in which tasks hold them; one stated order without a cycle, or a facility that acquires several locks together, removes it (STND.CONC.7). In one study of deadlocks, 22 percent came from a task acquiring a lock that it already held. Code under a lock that calls code whose locking it does not know, waits for another event, or suspends, holds the lock across steps that it does not control, and a callback is such code (STND.CONC.8). A wait for a condition is able to return when the condition does not hold, so it tests the condition again (STND.CONC.9). A wait on another task without a bound turns a lost notification into a hang, and the bound or the cancellation of STND.CONC.10 turns it into a report; a blocking read of a file or a device is outside it. A construct whose tasks the runtime is free to run one after another, a parallel loop among them, deadlocks when one of its tasks waits for another that has not started, so the third sentence of STND.CONC.11 keeps such waits out (c4, hazard 6). STND.CONC.11 closes the gap G3 beyond MPI: DSGN.15 asks for invariants, which are properties of safety, and a protocol in which tasks wait on one another also needs completion, which STND.MPI.12 asks only of message protocols. Where the statement of a protocol carries no argument of completion, the detection of deadlock of STND.CONC.25 is the evidence that remains. Message passing does not give freedom from deadlock by construction; in one study of 171 defects of a language built around channels, message passing caused more blocking defects than shared memory did (c2, section 3).
 
-The lifecycle rules follow the practice that the research note calls structured concurrency: a task does not outlive the scope that starts it, so its failure has somewhere to go and its resources have an end (STND.CONC.12; c2, section 4). The languages that provide scoped tasks, task groups, or threads that join when their handle ends make this the default; detached work is the exception. Cancellation that does not reach the tasks a task started leaves them running after their result is no longer wanted, and code that swallows a cancellation keeps its caller from ending (STND.CONC.13). A queue without a bound lets work accumulate without limit behind a slow consumer, which the literature on backpressure warns against (c2, section 4), and tasks that wait on one another inside one bounded pool deadlock when each worker holds a task that waits for a task still queued (STND.CONC.14). Code that blocks an event loop, or computes on it without returning control, delays every task on it, and the objects of most event loops are not safe to call from other threads (STND.CONC.15). A runtime that moves a task between threads at a suspension or scheduling point changes the thread on which the task resumes, so state that the task keeps in storage local to its thread, or selects by the identity of its thread, is lost or shared (STND.CONC.16); STND.JL.27 states the same of Julia, and the directive-based threading standard has a form for its tasks.
+The lifecycle rules follow the practice that the research note calls structured concurrency: a task does not outlive the scope that starts it, so its failure has somewhere to go and its resources have an end (STND.CONC.12; c2, section 4). The languages that provide scoped tasks, task groups, or threads that join when their handle ends make this the default; detached work is the exception. Cancellation that does not reach the tasks a task started leaves them running after their result is no longer wanted, and code that swallows a cancellation keeps its caller from ending (STND.CONC.13). A queue without a bound lets work accumulate without limit behind a slow consumer, which the literature on backpressure warns against (c2, section 4), and tasks that wait on one another inside one bounded pool deadlock when each worker holds a task that waits for a task still queued (STND.CONC.14). Code that blocks an event loop, or computes on it without returning control, delays every task on it, and the objects of most event loops are not safe to call from other threads (STND.CONC.15). A runtime that moves a task between threads at a suspension or scheduling point changes the thread on which the task resumes, so state that the task keeps in storage local to its thread, or selects by the identity of its thread, is lost or shared (STND.CONC.16); STND.JL.27 states the same of Julia, and STND.OMP.7 is its form for the tasks of OpenMP.
 
 The rules on state that applies to a whole process or node close the gap G4 beyond MPI (c1, item 8; c3, section 6). A library that creates its own pool of threads, or resizes a pool of the process, multiplies the threads of a program that already runs its own, and a library that changes the handling of signals or another setting of a runtime that applies to the whole process changes the behavior of its caller (STND.CONC.17). Two components that each size their pool to the whole node run twice as many threads as the node holds, which HW.CPU.5 reports and STND.CONC.18 prevents by having one place, the program, set the sizes. A process that creates a child by copying itself while other threads run gives the child a copy of locks that those threads held and that nothing in the child releases; POSIX restricts the child of a process with several threads to calls that are safe in a signal handler until it starts a new program (unverified; the research notes did not retrieve the POSIX text), so a child is started by a facility that starts a new program in it, or by one that the documentation of each runtime in the process states as safe (STND.CONC.19).
 
